/*  A book of account, carrying its own type.

    The point of this file is the `charge` type. It is declared INSIDE the book, which is the only
    place a type can be put where another file will ever see it — a file's top level is private to
    that file, and an object body otherwise takes only methods.

    Members are what an object HAS; declarations are what a module CARRIES, and a book is a module.
    A type is neither reached with `'s` nor requirable by an interface, so it is not a member, and
    only a module may hold one.

    ★ Nothing here pulls `math`, and `rounded-total` uses it anyway. A module's methods run inside
    the block that pulled the module, so they inherit what THAT block pulled — the debt is recorded
    here and settled at the pull. Run ledger.cufe, which is where it gets paid.  */

/// Charges, and what they add up to.
///
/// ★ It carries the `charge` type, so `Pull a book on bookkeeping.` brings `charge` into scope for
/// the length of the block — that is what lets a caller build the series these take.
///
/// ⚠ `rounded-total` uses `math` WITHOUT pulling it, so the block that pulls this book has to
/// supply it: `Pull a book on math.` around `Pull a book on bookkeeping.` See ledger.cufe.
Define object bookkeeping with () and book:
    Define object charge with (the text note, the number amount):
        Bind text to line:
            Return "{one's note}: {one's amount}".
        Done.
    Done.

    /// Every charge added together, exactly.
    Bind number to total, given (the series of charge charges):
        Define sum as 0.
        For each item in charges, repeat:
            Increment sum by item's amount.
        Done.
        Return sum.
    Done.

    /// The same total, to whole pennies.
    ///
    /// ⚠ This is the member that needs `math`, and it does not pull it — see the book's note.
    Bind number to rounded-total, given (the series of charge charges):
        Define exact as cast one's total on (charges).
        Return (cast math's round on (exact * 100)) / 100.
    Done.
Done.
